    <body>
      <sec id="s1"><title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Boilerplate learning objectives that must be skipped.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Objective one, also skipped.</p></list-item></list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="s2"><title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Lactic acidosis is a raised blood lactate with a low blood pH.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b1">[1]</xref> In critically ill adults it most often reflects inadequate oxygen delivery to tissues.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Its severity is usually graded by the lactate concentration in <italic>mmol/L</italic>.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="s3"><title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Causes are grouped as follows:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item><p>Type A: tissue hypoperfusion, as in septic or cardiogenic shock.</p></list-item>
          <list-item><p>Type B: impaired lactate clearance, as in liver failure or with some drugs.</p></list-item>
        </list>
        <fig id="f1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>A synthetic figure caption that must be skipped.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="fig1.jpg"/></fig>
        <table-wrap id="t1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>A synthetic table caption.</p></caption><table><tr><td>cell text to skip</td></tr></table></table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="s4"><title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Treatment targets the cause and restores perfusion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b4">[4]</xref></p>
        <sec id="s4a"><title>Fluids and Vasopressors</title>
          <p>Fluids are given first; vasopressors are added when hypotension persists ( <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b5">5</xref> ).</p>
        </sec>
      </sec>
